.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt ZONEFILE.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zonefile.conf
.Nd zonefile-go configuration file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
describes forward zones, their hosts and records, the networks for
which
.Xr zonefile-go 8
generates reverse zones, and the subnets it writes a
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5
for.
.Pp
The file usually has these sections, in this order:
.Bl -tag -width xxxx
.It Sy Macros
User-defined variables that may be used later in the file.
.It Sy Global configuration
Defaults for all zones, and default nameservers and mail exchangers.
.It Sy Reverse zones
The networks for which PTR records are served.
.It Sy DHCP
The IPv4 subnets served by
.Xr dhcpd 8 .
.It Sy Zones
The forward zones with their hosts, aliases and records.
.El
.Pp
Macros may be defined and
.Ic include
may be used anywhere at top level, and
.Ic zone
and
.Ic reverse
blocks may follow each other in any order.
Only the global settings must come before the first
.Ic zone
or
.Ic reverse
block.
.Pp
Each statement ends at the end of the line.
A backslash at the very end of a line continues the statement on the next
line.
Comments start with
.Sq #
and run to the end of the line.
Words are separated by whitespace; a value containing spaces or special
characters has to be enclosed in double quotes, in which
.Sq \e\(dq
and
.Sq \e\e
are the only escapes.
.Pp
Lists are enclosed in curly braces and may span several lines; commas
between their items are optional:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
alias { www, ftp
        mail }
.Ed
.Pp
A block, such as
.Ic zone ,
opens with a curly brace at the end of the line and closes with one on a
line of its own.
.Pp
Durations are given in seconds, or as numbers with the units
.Cm s ,
.Cm m ,
.Cm h ,
.Cm d
and
.Cm w ,
as in
.Cm 3h
or
.Cm 1h30m .
.Sh MACROS
A macro is defined as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
name = value
.Ed
.Pp
and used as
.Ar $name .
Macro names consist of letters, digits and underscores, do not start with
a digit and must not be keywords.
As in
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
the content of a quoted value is split into words again, so that
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lan = "{ 192.168.21.0/24 fd00:1234:5678:1000::/64 }"
.Ed
.Pp
defines a list.
Macros are expanded where they are defined, and a reference must form a
whole word:
.Ar $prefix::1
is not expanded.
.Nm zonefile-go Fl n
notes macros that are defined but never used.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic include Ar file
Read
.Ar file ,
which must be quoted, at this point.
A relative path is resolved against the directory of the including file.
.El
.Sh GLOBAL CONFIGURATION
Settings give the values of a zone.
At top level they are the defaults for all zones; inside a
.Ic zone
or
.Ic reverse
block they override them for that block and must come before its first
record.
Each setting may be given once per scope.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic email Ar address
Contact address for the SOA record.
Required.
.It Ic ttl Ar duration
Default TTL of the zone and its records; default 3h.
.It Ic refresh Ar duration
SOA refresh; default 2h.
.It Ic retry Ar duration
SOA retry; default 1h.
Must be less than
.Ic refresh .
.It Ic expire Ar duration
SOA expire; default 2w.
.It Ic negative-ttl Ar duration
SOA minimum, the TTL of negative answers; default 1h.
.It Ic serial Ar number
A fixed serial instead of the computed one.
.It Oo Ic no Oc Ic ptr
Whether hosts get PTR records; by default they do.
At top level this sets the default for all zones; inside a
.Ic zone
it overrides it for that zone.
It follows the same ordering rule as the settings above and is not
allowed in
.Ic reverse
blocks.
.It Ic nameserver Ar name | { name ... } Op Ic ttl Ar duration
At top level, the nameservers of every zone that declares none of its
own.
The first nameserver is the primary of the SOA record.
At top level the names must be absolute, that is end in a dot.
.It Ic mx Ar name Oo Ic priority Ar number Oc Op Ic ttl Ar duration
At top level, a mail exchanger for every zone that declares none of its
own and does not say
.Ic no mx .
The name must be absolute.
.El
.Sh REVERSE ZONES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic reverse Ar network | { network ... } Op { ... }
Declare one reverse zone per network, named after it:
192.168.0.0/16 becomes 168.192.in-addr.arpa.
The prefix length must be a multiple of 8 for IPv4 and of 4 for IPv6, and
the networks must not overlap.
.Pp
Every address of a host with PTR records enabled gets a PTR record in the
reverse zone whose network contains it.
Addresses outside every reverse network get none, so external addresses
in forward zones never lead to PTR records for zones that are not served.
.Pp
The optional block may contain the settings
.Ic email ,
.Ic ttl ,
.Ic refresh ,
.Ic retry ,
.Ic expire ,
.Ic negative-ttl
and
.Ic serial ,
and
.Ic nameserver
statements with absolute names.
.El
.Sh ZONES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic zone Ar name No { ... }
Declare a forward zone; the trailing dot of
.Ar name
is optional.
.El
.Pp
Inside a zone, a name that does not end in a dot is relative to the zone,
.Sq @
is the zone itself and
.Sq *
a wildcard label.
A relative name that repeats the zone name or ends in a top-level domain,
such as
.Sq mail.home.arpa ,
gets a warning, since it was probably meant to be absolute.
A zone may contain:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic email , ttl , refresh , retry , expire , negative-ttl , serial
.It Oo Ic no Oc Ic ptr
As in the global configuration.
.It Oo Ic no Oc Ic dhcp
Whether hosts of the zone with a
.Ic mac
get a fixed address in the
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5 ,
see
.Sx DHCP ;
by default they do not.
Hosts without a
.Ic mac
are left out.
Like the settings, it must come before the first record.
.It Ic dhcp-profile Ar name
The default profile of the hosts of the zone, see
.Sx DHCP ;
it must come before the first record.
.It Ic dhcp Oo Ar network Oc No { ... }
A
.Ic dhcp
block, see
.Sx DHCP ,
for
.Ar network ,
or without it for the IPv4 network of the zone, which a
.Ic network
statement must declare before it.
Its
.Ic option domain-name
is the zone unless the block gives one, and it turns
.Ic dhcp
on for the hosts of the zone;
.Ic no dhcp
in the zone or on a host wins.
A zone may have one such block without a network, and any number with
one.
.It Ic no inet | no inet6
Drop the IPv4 or IPv6 addresses of all hosts of the zone, whether
suffixes, full addresses or taken from other hosts.
A host turns the family on again with
.Ic inet
or
.Ic inet6 .
Not both in one zone; it must come before the first record.
.It Ic no mx
The zone has no MX records, not even the top-level ones.
Not allowed together with
.Ic mx
in the same zone.
.It Ic network Ar network | { network ... }
The networks that address suffixes of this zone are resolved against: at
most one IPv4 and one IPv6 network.
Must come before the first
.Ic host .
.It Ic nameserver Ar name | { name ... } Op Ic ttl Ar duration
The nameservers of the zone.
.It Ic mx Ar name Oo Ic priority Ar number Oc Op Ic ttl Ar duration
A mail exchanger of the zone.
The default priority is 0, here and at top level.
.It Xo
.Ic host Ar name address | { address ... }
.Op Ic alias Ar name | { name ... }
.Op Ic ttl Ar duration
.Op Oo Ic no Oc Ic ptr
.Op Oo Ic no Oc Ic inet
.Op Oo Ic no Oc Ic inet6
.Op Ic mac Ar address | { address ... }
.Op Oo Ic no Oc Ic dhcp
.Op Ic dhcp-profile Ar name
.Op { ... }
.Xc
Create one A or AAAA record per address.
The options may also be given in a block after the addresses, one or more
per line; each still only once.
An address may also be the absolute name of a host or alias of the
configuration, ending in a dot, such as
.Sq unifi.home.arpa. ;
the host then gets its A and AAAA records, without PTR records.
.Ic no inet
and
.Ic no inet6
apply to them as well.
A name without addresses, or one that takes its own addresses from
another host, is an error.
An address is a full IPv4 or IPv6 address, or a suffix such as
.Cm .37
or
.Cm .21.37 ,
which yields one address per network of the zone: the suffix is read as a
number and placed into the host part of the network, so that
.Cm .37
becomes 192.168.21.37 in 192.168.21.0/24 and fd00::25 in fd00::/64.
A zone with only an IPv4 network therefore gets only A records from
suffixes.
.Pp
Each
.Ic alias
gets the same addresses as additional A and AAAA records, but no PTR
record.
.Ic no inet
and
.Ic no inet6
drop the addresses of that family, for hosts without IPv6 in a zone with
both networks;
.Ic inet
and
.Ic inet6
keep them against a
.Ic no inet
or
.Ic no inet6
of the zone.
.Ic ptr
and
.Ic no ptr
override the setting of the zone for this host.
Wildcard hosts never get PTR records.
.Pp
.Ic mac
records the MAC addresses of the host, written as six hexadecimal octets
such as 00:00:5e:00:53:01; each may be used only once in the
configuration.
On its own it changes no output.
.Ic dhcp
and
.Ic no dhcp
override the setting of the zone for this host.
With
.Ic dhcp ,
the host gets a fixed address in the
.Ic dhcp
block whose network holds its IPv4 address, see
.Sx DHCP ;
it needs a
.Ic mac ,
and it is an error if there is no such block.
.Ic dhcp-profile
puts the host into a profile of that block and overrides the profile of
the zone.
.It Ic cname Ar name target Op Ic ttl Ar duration
A CNAME record.
Its name must not be used by any other record of the zone.
.It Xo
.Ic srv Ar _service._proto target Ic port Ar number
.Op Ic priority Ar number
.Op Ic weight Ar number
.Op Ic ttl Ar duration
.Xc
An SRV record.
The default priority is 5, the default weight 10.
.El
.Pp
A nameserver whose name lies in a zone of the configuration must have an
A or AAAA record in that zone and must not be a CNAME.
.Pp
Records can only be created within the zone: a host, alias,
.Ic cname
or
.Ic srv
with an absolute name outside it is an error.
Targets, such as that of a
.Ic cname ,
may lie anywhere.
.Pp
Within a zone, a host must not be declared twice, and the same name with
the same address must not result twice.
Two hosts producing a PTR record for the same address in a reverse zone is
an error as well.
.Sh DHCP
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic dhcp Ar network No { ... }
Describe an IPv4 subnet for
.Xr dhcpd 8 .
.Xr zonefile-go 8
writes the complete
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5
from these blocks with
.Fl t Cm dhcpd .
Addresses in the block may be suffixes relative to
.Ar network .
Subnets must not overlap.
The statements are named as in
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5
and
.Xr dhcp-options 5 ,
but take addresses as suffixes, lists in braces and durations as above,
and end without a semicolon.
The block may contain:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic range Ar low high
A range of addresses handed out dynamically.
It may be given several times; ranges must lie within the network and
must not overlap.
.It Ic server-identifier Ar address
The IPv4 address of this server that
.Xr dhcpd 8
sends to the clients, which they use to address it, e.g. to renew a
lease.
Only needed if the default, the first address of the interface the request
arrived on, is not the right one, as with several addresses on one
interface or a
.Xr carp 4
address.
Not allowed in a profile.
.It Oo Ic not Oc Ic authoritative
Whether
.Xr dhcpd 8
answers requests for addresses that are wrong for the network with a
DHCPNAK; by default it does.
Not allowed in a profile.
.It Ic option routers Ar address | { address ... }
The default gateways; the addresses must lie within the network.
.It Ic option domain-name-servers Ar address | { address ... }
The resolvers of the clients.
.It Ic option ntp-servers Ar address | { address ... }
The NTP servers.
.It Ic option smtp-server Ar address | { address ... }
The SMTP servers.
.It Ic option domain-name Ar name
The domain of the clients.
.It Ic option domain-search Ar name | { name ... }
The search list of the clients.
.It Ic option autoproxy-script Ar url
The URL of the proxy configuration for the Web Proxy Autodiscovery
Protocol.
.It Ic get-lease-hostnames Cm true | false
With
.Cm true ,
.Xr dhcpd 8
looks up a name for every address of the dynamic ranges and hands it out
as host name; addresses without a PTR record get a warning from
.Xr dhcpd 8 .
By default it does not.
.It Ic default-lease-time Ar duration
The lease time if the client asks for none.
.It Ic max-lease-time Ar duration
The longest lease time;
.Ic default-lease-time
must not be longer.
.Pp
Other options of
.Xr dhcp-options 5
are not supported.
.It Ic dhcp-profile Ar name No { ... }
Options for the hosts that name the profile with
.Ic dhcp-profile
in their line or zone:
any of the statements above except
.Ic range ,
.Ic server-identifier
and
.Ic authoritative .
Each name may be used once per block.
A profile in a
.Ic dhcp
block hides a global profile of the same name for its network.
A profile that is used becomes a
.Ic group
inside the subnet, whose options override those of the subnet for its
hosts.
.El
.It Ic dhcp No { ... }
The global
.Ic dhcp
block, without a network, holds the defaults of all
.Ic dhcp
blocks; there may be one.
It takes the same statements as a
.Ic dhcp
block except
.Ic range .
Its suffixes are resolved for each subnet, so
.Ic option domain-name-servers .1
names the address .1 of every network.
Profiles in it serve all networks.
.Pp
Options are inherited one by one: the global block, then the
.Ic dhcp
block, then the profile of a host; each level replaces the options it
gives, lists as a whole.
The checks apply to the result for each subnet.
In the
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5 ,
the settings of the global block without suffixes stand at the top level,
from where
.Xr dhcpd 8
passes them on; lists with suffixes are resolved and written into each
subnet that does not give its own.
.Pp
Every host with a
.Ic mac
and
.Ic dhcp
on, given on the host or in its zone, gets a host declaration in the subnet that holds its IPv4 address, with
.Ic hardware ethernet ,
.Ic fixed-address
and
.Ic option host-name
set to the first label of its name.
A host with several MAC addresses gets one declaration per address; the
further ones are named
.Ar name Ns -2 ,
.Ar name Ns -3
and so on.
It is an error if such a host has no IPv4 address in the network of a
.Ic dhcp
block, if its fixed address lies in a dynamic range, or if its
.Ic dhcp-profile
is defined neither in that block nor in the global one.
While dhcp is off for a host, its
.Ic dhcp-profile
is kept unused; if no
.Ic dhcp
block defines the name at all, zonefile-go warns.
.Pp
.Nm zonefile-go Fl n
also shows notes about profiles that hide a global one or that no host
uses, about
.Ic dhcp
blocks with neither a
.Ic range
nor a host with
.Ic dhcp ,
which serve no client, and about addresses in
.Ic option domain-name-servers
that are not a nameserver of the zone named by
.Ic option domain-name ,
if zonefile-go manages that zone and the addresses of all its
nameservers.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/zonefile.conf
Default configuration file.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/examples/zonefile-go/zonefile.conf
Example configuration file.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lan = "{ 192.168.1.0/24 fd00:1::/64 }"

email hostmaster@example.com
nameserver ns1.example.com.

reverse { 192.168.1.0/24 fd00:1::/64 }

dhcp 192.168.1.0/24 {
	range .100 .199
	option routers .1
	option domain-name-servers .1
	option domain-name example.com
	default-lease-time 1d
}

zone example.com {
	network $lan
	mx mail priority 10

	host router .1 alias { @ ns1 www }
	host mail   .3
	host printer .12 no inet6 mac 00:00:5e:00:53:12 dhcp
	host gateway 203.0.113.1 no ptr

	cname webmail mail
	srv _imaps._tcp mail port 993
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dhcp-options 5 ,
.Xr dhcpd.conf 5 ,
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
.Xr dhcpd 8 ,
.Xr zonefile-go 8
